content:
Looking at your screenshot and the code, I can see two distinct issues:

  Issue 1: Interior tile mismatch across transition boundaries

  In tile-painter.js line 69-80, getWangSrc() does this:
  - Edge tiles (near a transition): uses tile.transitionPair.dir — e.g., shallow_water_to_beach
  - Interior tiles (deep in a biome): uses BIOME_INTERIOR[tile.biome] — e.g., lake_to_shallow_water

  So shallow_water near beach uses shallow_water_to_beach__wang_6, but shallow_water near lake uses lake_to_shallow_water__wang_12. These are from different PixelLab generations with different colors. That's
  what creates the visible bands.

  Issue 2: The palette fill shows through

  Lines 96-104 paint a solid color fill first, then overlay the wang PNG on top. Since the wang PNGs are opaque 32x32 tiles, the palette fill shouldn't matter for edge tiles — but the color mismatch between
  the wang PNG and palette can create visible edges at tile boundaries.

  Instructions for WizardGenie

  Fix 1: Use transition-consistent interiors (eliminates the bands)

  Change getWangSrc() so that interior tiles always use the same tileset as the nearest transition. Instead of falling back to BIOME_INTERIOR, the interior tile should use the from or to interior tile from
  whatever transition pair is active in the region:

  function getWangSrc(tile) {
    var mask = tile.wangEdgeMask;
    if (mask === undefined) mask = 0;

    if (tile.transitionPair) {
      // Edge tile — use transition tileset with computed mask
      return TRANSITIONS_BASE + tile.transitionPair.dir + '/wang/' +
             tile.transitionPair.dir + '__wang_' + mask + WANG_SUFFIX;
    }

    // Interior tile — use the SAME tileset as nearest transition
    // wang_6 = "to" biome interior, wang_12 = "from" biome interior
    if (tile.nearestTransitionPair) {
      var intMask = (tile.nearestTransitionSide === 'from') ? 12 : 6;
      return TRANSITIONS_BASE + tile.nearestTransitionPair.dir + '/wang/' +
             tile.nearestTransitionPair.dir + '__wang_' + intMask + WANG_SUFFIX;
    }

    // Fallback for tiles with no nearby transition at all
    var interior = BIOME_INTERIOR[tile.biome];
    if (interior) {
      return TRANSITIONS_BASE + interior.dir + '/wang/' +
             interior.dir + '__wang_' + interior.mask + WANG_SUFFIX;
    }
    return null;
  }

  This requires propagating nearestTransitionPair and nearestTransitionSide into the tile data during chunk compilation — which the ±8 radius scan in chunk-render-cache.js lines 186-199 already partially does.

  Fix 2: Blending techniques to smooth hard edges

  Even with Fix 1, you'll get seam lines where two different transition regions meet. Here are techniques to hide them:

  A. Alpha-fade at region boundaries:
  When a tile's nearestTransitionPair differs from an adjacent tile's, draw both wang tiles and alpha-blend between them:

  // If neighbor uses a different tileset, cross-fade
  if (neighborTransitionDir !== tileTransitionDir) {
    ctx.globalAlpha = 0.5;
    paintWangBase(ctx, neighborTile, sx, sy, size); // paint neighbor's version
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1.0;
    paintWangBase(ctx, tile, sx, sy, size); // paint own version on top
  }

  B. Surface overlay masking:
  Your surface_overlays/ already have scatter tiles (vegetation, debris, texture). Draw these at higher opacity along seam boundaries to visually break up the hard edge. The overlays are biome-themed and will
  naturally tie the colors together.

  C. Palette tint unification:
  In paintTerrainTile() lines 96-104, the palette fill already provides a base tint. You could use ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply' or 'overlay' when drawing the wang PNG to blend it with the palette
  color, which would unify the hues:

  ctx.fillStyle = shaded;
  ctx.fillRect(sx, sy, size, size);
  ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply'; // or 'overlay'
  paintWangBase(ctx, tile, sx, sy, size);
  ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over'; // reset

  D. Noise-based dithering at seams:
  Use your existing coherentPatch() noise to probabilistically choose which tileset to sample from near seam boundaries. This creates a naturally irregular transition instead of a hard line.

  My Recommendation

  Start with Fix 1 — it eliminates the worst bands by ensuring continuity within transition regions. This is a code-only change, no new assets needed.

  Then add surface overlays (technique B) to dress up the remaining seams. You already have ~1,464 overlay tiles. Layer 2-3 overlays per tile and the color variation becomes a feature ("natural variation in
  terrain") rather than a bug.

  The palette tint unification (C) is a nice quick win too — one line change that harmonizes all wang tiles with the biome's canonical color scheme.

